From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Playport <dev@playport.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 07:59:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WOW64: leave a guest's read fault in an RWX interval to the
 guest

The invalidation tracker write-protects the pages of an RWX interval
that hold compiled code; such a page stays readable. Portal 2's audio
thread read uncommitted memory inside an RWX interval, the module
claimed the fault as self-modifying code, the unprotect could not make
the page readable, and the read faulted again forever until the host
ended the app. Consult the tracker for write faults only, so a read
fault becomes the guest's access violation.

Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-04-portal2-d3d9-vulkan.md
Offered-upstream: no; the fault loop needs the iOS window
---
 Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp b/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp
index f08ffd1..e6a1928 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/WOW64/Module.cpp
@@ -1072,7 +1072,10 @@ bool BTCpuResetToConsistentStateImpl(EXCEPTION_POINTERS* Ptrs) {
       return true;
     }
 
-    if (Thread) {
+    // Only a write can hit a page the tracker trapped for self-modifying code: a
+    // trapped page stays readable. A read fault in an RWX interval is the guest's
+    // own (uncommitted memory, a bad pointer); claiming it would retry it forever.
+    if (Thread && Exception->ExceptionInformation[0] == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT) {
       std::scoped_lock Lock(ThreadCreationMutex);
       FEXCORE_PROFILE_INSTANT_INCREMENT(Thread, AccumulatedSMCCount, 1);
       if (InvalidationTracker->HandleRWXAccessViolation(Thread, Context->Pc, Window::ToHost(FaultAddress))) {
